  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 100 PM Saturday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... While some frost will still be possible tonight, \nadditional cloud cover should prevent lows from getting as cold as \npreviously forecast.\n\nFor a few days, tonight has looked to be the coldest night out of \nthe next several nights, and that remains the case. The limiting \nfactors for frost potential were always going to be whether the wind \nwould go calm and how much high cloud cover there would be, both of \nwhich could be limiting factors. While the wind still appears that \nit will be on the light side, which is not much of a change, the \nlatest RRFS shows quite a bit more cloud cover than previous \nforecast runs. With the additional high clouds, most guidance has \nbrought overnight lows up by a few degrees, enough of a change that \nit appears a Frost Advisory is not warranted at this time. Still \ncan't rule out some patchy frost in the usual cold spots, and if \nlater runs of the RRFS indicate that high clouds will be limited, a \nFrost Advisory might be needed.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Rain chances and amounts appear insignificant \nthrough the forecast period. Below normal temperatures and potential \nfor widespread frost/freeze next weekend.\n\nThe forecast will likely remain dry through midweek as central NC \nwill be influenced by mostly anticyclonic northwest mid/upper-lvl \nflow and disturbances within the southern stream remaining confined \nto the Gulf Coast and Deep South through Wed. A highly amplified \nmid/upper-lvl pattern is expected to evolve late week into the \nweekend as the polar jet stream digs south and phases with the sub-\ntropical jet on the east side of a developing Omega blocking \nconfiguration Wed night into Fri before shifting east through the \nweekend.\n\nDeep-layer moisture return appears very limited in ensemble \nsolutions with rich Gulf moisture mostly directed across FL and into \nthe western Atlantic. Coupled with the general consensus of the \ntrack of the associated surface low remaining north of the forecast \narea (Ohio Valley across the central Appalachians and off the \nDelmarva) impactful/beneficial rainfall appears unlikely. Reasonable \nhigh-end amounts in 24 hours from 00z LREF are below 0.5\" between \nThurs and Friday afternoon. \n\nHigher confidence exists in arrival of below normal temperatures \nlate week into the weekend as low-lvl thickness fall into the 1320-\n1300m range from available deterministic guidance. This would favor \nthe best chance for the first widespread frost/freeze of the season \nas early as Fri morning, but more probable subsequent mornings over \nthe weekend into next week as surface high becomes more favorably \npositioned and long-range cloud cover forecast favors mostly clear \nskies for optimal radiational cooling.",
